      "This project focused on designing the first region-wide resilient Marine Protected Area network for the Arafura and Timor Seas, a transboundary marine region shared by Indonesia, Timor-Leste, Australia, and Papua New Guinea. The region contains highly valuable coastal and marine ecosystems, including coral reefs, seagrass beds, mangroves, estuaries, deepwater habitats, migratory corridors, turtle nesting beaches, and culturally important marine areas. The project was published in Coastal Management under the title A Resilient Marine Protected Area Network Design: A First for the Arafura Timor Seas. The main goal was to identify gaps in the existing protected area system and propose a more connected, representative, and resilient MPA network across the entire ATS region. Although each country already had existing or proposed MPAs, these areas were not originally designed as one integrated regional network. This created a need for a broader spatial planning approach that could account for transboundary ecological features, fisheries connectivity, migratory species, climate change vulnerability, socioeconomic uses, and marine threats. The project used a combination of GIS-based marine gap analysis and systematic conservation planning. First, existing MPAs, proposed MPAs, and previously identified Areas of Interest were compiled into a regional geospatial database. The planning area was then stratified by country and marine ecoregion to ensure that conservation targets were assessed across both political and ecological boundaries. This was important because a network that looks good statistically can still be useless if it protects everything in one corner and ignores the rest. A wide range of conservation features were included in the analysis. These covered shallow-water habitats such as coral reefs, seagrasses, mangroves, and estuaries, as well as coral reef habitat classes, deepwater geomorphic features, seamounts, canyons, turtle nesting beaches, migratory corridors, dive sites, tourism areas, shipwrecks, aircraft wrecks, local wisdom areas, and Aboriginal heritage areas. The map on page 8 shows the shallow-water conservation features, while the map on page 10 highlights important areas for endangered, threatened, and protected species, including sea turtle, whale shark, cetacean, and seabird-related areas. The analysis also considered human uses and threats that could conflict with conservation planning. These included fishing pressure, shipping lanes, ports, oil and gas areas, underwater cables, military areas, sea mine areas, power plant impacts, mangrove logging, turtle egg poaching, blast fishing, poison fishing, reef gleaning, and shark finning. A weighted cost surface was developed to help identify priority conservation areas while minimizing conflict with fisheries, industry, transportation, and other existing marine uses. The fishing pressure and threat maps on page 12 and the weighted cost surface on page 13 are especially strong visuals for explaining this part of the workflow. The project used Marxan, a systematic conservation planning tool, to identify priority areas for inclusion in the regional MPA network. The Marxan analysis was performed in two stages: first for shallow-water habitats using finer planning units, and then for combined shallow and deepwater habitats using larger planning units. Existing MPAs, proposed MPAs, and suitable AOIs were locked into the analysis, while incompatible areas such as military zones, ports, sea mine areas, and some industrial-use zones were locked out. This allowed the model to search for efficient conservation solutions while respecting real-world planning constraints. A major strength of the project was that it combined technical spatial analysis with stakeholder consultation. The process involved national and regional workshops with governments, scientists, NGOs, women’s groups, private-sector actors, and other stakeholders from the four ATS countries. Their input was used to review data layers, refine assumptions, validate results, and improve the final MPA network design. In other words, the output was not just a “GIS says so” map; it was a planning product shaped through both spatial evidence and regional consultation. The final ATS MPA Network design covered 301,055 km² and included 93 existing and proposed MPAs, 13 previously identified Areas of Interest, and seven new Areas of Interest identified through this study. The seven new AOIs included three in Indonesia, two in Timor-Leste, and two in Australia. One of the most important proposals was the Motamasin AOI, designed as a potential transboundary MPA between Indonesia and Timor-Leste. The results showed that the proposed network would improve representation of key conservation features across the ATS region, especially shallow-water habitats and critical ecological areas. However, the analysis also showed that some targets remained difficult to achieve, particularly for estuaries, some deepwater habitats, seamounts, canyons, migratory corridors, and wreck sites. In many cases, these gaps were caused by overlap with existing marine uses such as oil and gas concessions, underwater cables, ports, shipping lanes, and areas with high fishing pressure. This makes the project especially valuable because it demonstrates the real tension between conservation priorities and marine spatial planning constraints. Overall, this project demonstrates how GIS, conservation planning, stakeholder engagement, and spatial decision-support tools can be used to design a practical and regionally coordinated MPA network. The work contributed to a conservation planning framework that can support biodiversity protection, fisheries resilience, climate adaptation, and transboundary marine governance across one of the most ecologically important marine regions in Southeast Asia and northern Australia.",
